{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cd4wKQEUad3uGsSwDnEdy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 6 scenarios pass — the 2 new limit scenarios plus the 4 existing reservation scenarios. `BUILD SUCCESS`, no failures or errors.\n\n## What I changed\n\nTwo files, both in `src/main/java/com/thinking/ticket/` — nothing in `provided` or `src/test/`, and the contract's entry point and constructor are untouched.\n\n**`TicketLimitExceededException.java`** (new) — a `RuntimeException` matching the style of the existing exceptions.\n\n**`TicketService.java`** — added the limit check right after the user lookup and before the charge, so a rejected reservation never bills the card:\n\n```java\nif (tickets.countByUserId(userId) >= TICKETS_PER_USER) {\n    throw new TicketLimitExceededException(...);\n}\n```\n\nI put the check in the service rather than in `Ticket` because the rule is about the member's holdings, not about any one ticket — the same reason the user-existence check already lives there. The limit is a named constant (`TICKETS_PER_USER = 1`) rather than a bare `1`, since \"one per person\" is a policy number likely to move.\n\n## One thing worth knowing\n\nYour first `mvn test` run would have failed, but not because of this change. `target/test-classes/features/` still holds a `point_payment.feature` from some earlier exercise that no longer exists in `src/`. Maven's resource plugin copies files in but never deletes ones that have been removed, so Cucumber kept discovering it and erroring on four undefined steps. `mvn clean test` clears it. If you see those point/포인트 step errors again, it's the stale artifact, not your code — and it'll come back whenever you run without `clean`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":46800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_to